            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "steps",
          "title": "Binary → hexadecimal",
          "items": [
            "Split the binary number into groups of **4 bits from the right**.",
            "Pad the leftmost group with leading zeros if needed.",
            "Replace each group with its hex digit."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "content": "- 1011 0111₂ → **B7₁₆**\n- 11 1110 1001₂ → 0011 1110 1001 → **3E9₁₆**\n- Hex → binary: **2F₁₆** → 0010 1111₂"
        },
        {
          "type": "example",
          "title": "Checking via denary",
          "content": "B7₁₆ = 11 × 16 + 7 = **183₁₀**, and 10110111₂ = 128 + 32 + 16 + 4 + 2 + 1 = **183₁₀** ✓"
        },
        {
          "type": "interactive",
          "widget": "base_converter",
          "title": "Convert between bases"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Why hexadecimal is used",
      "objectives": [
        "12.1.1.2"
      ],
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Uses of hexadecimal",
          "columns": [
            "Use",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Memory addresses",
              "`0x7FFE4A10` in debuggers and error messages"
            ],
            [
              "Colour codes (24-bit RGB)",
              "`#1F4FD8` = red 1F, green 4F, blue D8"
            ],
            [
              "MAC addresses",
              "`3C:52:82:1A:9F:04`"
            ],
            [
              "Machine code and memory dumps",
              "`B8 04 00 CD 21`"
            ],
            [
              "Error and status codes",
              "Windows \"0x80070005\""
            ],
            [
              "IPv6 addresses",
              "`2001:0db8::1`"
            ],
            [
              "Character codes",
              "Unicode `U+04D8` (Ә)"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "**Shorter** than binary — a byte is 2 hex digits instead of 8 bits.",
            "**Easier to read, remember and write** → fewer mistakes.",
            "**Easy to convert** to and from binary (unlike denary).",
            "Makes debugging and low-level programming quicker."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "warning",
          "content": "Hexadecimal does **not** save memory or make the computer faster — data is always stored in binary. Hex only helps **people** work with binary values."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
